# HYPNOTISM (DIGITAL MEDIA AND ONLINE SERVICES) ACT 2024

## An Act to extend and apply the provisions of the Hypnotism Act 1952 to online platforms, streamed media services, and digital broadcast content; to make provision for the regulation of hypnotic and hypnotically suggestive content transmitted by electronic means; to confer powers upon the Secretary of State and relevant regulatory authorities; and for connected purposes.

*Be it enacted by the King's most Excellent Majesty, by and with the advice and consent of the Lords Spiritual and Temporal, and Commons, in this present Parliament assembled, and by the authority of the same, as follows:—*

---

## PART 1

### PRELIMINARY

**Citation, commencement and extent**

**1.**—(1) This Act may be cited as the Hypnotism (Digital Media and Online Services) Act 2024.

(2) This Act comes into force on such day as the Secretary of State may by regulations made by statutory instrument appoint, and different days may be appointed for different provisions.

(3) This Act extends to England and Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland, subject to the provisions of section 47 below.

(4) Her Majesty's Government may, by Order in Council, extend the provisions of this Act, with such modifications as appear appropriate, to any of the Channel Islands, the Isle of Man, or any British Overseas Territory.

---

**Interpretation**

**2.**—(1) In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires—

**"the 1952 Act"** means the Hypnotism Act 1952;

**"algorithmic amplification"** means any automated computational process employed by a platform service by which content is selected, ranked, sequenced, promoted, or recommended to a user without that user having directly and specifically requested that particular content;

**"audio-visual content"** means any combination of moving or still images and sounds, or either of them alone, transmitted or made available by electronic means;

**"the Authority"** means the Office of Communications established under the Office of Communications Act 2002, as supplemented by the Communications Act 2003;

**"binaural audio technique"** means any audio production method involving the separate manipulation of sound delivered to each ear of a listener, whether by headphones or other means, with the purpose or likely effect of inducing an altered state of awareness or suggestibility in the listener;

**"covert hypnotic technique"** means any technique, method, or sequence of communications delivered through digital media which is designed or reasonably likely—

- (a) to bypass the critical faculties of a recipient without their awareness; or
- (b) to induce a state of heightened suggestibility through means not apparent to a reasonable person viewing or interacting with such content;

**"designated online service"** means any service falling within the categories set out in Schedule 1 to this Act;

**"digital media"** means audio-visual content, interactive content, or immersive content delivered by means of—

- (a) the internet;
- (b) a digital broadcasting network;
- (c) a satellite broadcasting service;
- (d) a cable programme service;
- (e) any on-demand programme service within the meaning of section 368A of the Communications Act 2003; or
- (f) any other electronic communications network within the meaning of the Communications Act 2003;

**"hypnotic induction content"** means content which—

- (a) contains or constitutes a hypnotic induction, whether complete or partial;
- (b) employs techniques known or reasonably likely to produce a hypnotic or trance-like state in a recipient; or
- (c) uses binaural audio techniques, repetitive visual patterns, guided progressive relaxation, or other methods associated with hypnotic practice, for the purpose of inducing altered states of consciousness;

**"immersive content"** includes content delivered by means of virtual reality, augmented reality, or mixed reality technologies;

**"interactive content"** means content with which a recipient may engage in a manner which adapts or responds to that recipient's inputs, choices, or biometric data;

**"live-streamed content"** means audio-visual content transmitted in real time or near-real time over a digital network to recipients;

**"operator"** means any person who provides, controls, manages, or is responsible for a designated online service, including a platform operator, content host, or content aggregator;

**"person"** includes a body corporate, an unincorporated association, and a partnership;

**"platform service"** has the meaning given by section 3 of this Act;

**"professional hypnotherapist"** means a person who holds a qualification in clinical or therapeutic hypnosis recognised by a body listed in Schedule 2 to this Act, and who practises hypnotherapy in a clinical, therapeutic, or educational context;

**"protective safeguard"** means any technical or procedural measure required under Part 3 of this Act;

**"recipient"** means any natural person who accesses, views, or otherwise receives digital media content, whether or not they have registered with or subscribed to a designated online service;

**"the Secretary of State"** means the Secretary of State for Science, Innovation and Technology, or such other Secretary of State as may be designated for the purposes of this Act by the Prime Minister;

**"subliminal content"** has the meaning given in section 4 of this Act;

**"therapeutic content"** means hypnotic induction content produced and delivered exclusively for clinical, psychological, or evidenced therapeutic purposes by or under the supervision of a professional hypnotherapist;

**"UK-established service"** has the meaning given by section 368Z7 of the Communications Act 2003 as if applied mutatis mutandis to the services described in this Act.

(2) Expressions used in this Act and also used in the Communications Act 2003 or the Online Safety Act 2023 shall, unless the contrary intention appears, have the same meaning as in those Acts.

(3) For the purposes of this Act, a reference to hypnotic techniques shall be construed broadly and shall include techniques derived from or associated with Ericksonian hypnosis, neurolinguistic programming where deployed for purposes of covert induction, guided visualisation, and any other analogous methodology, where such techniques are employed to induce a hypnotic or significantly altered state of consciousness.

---

**Platform services: scope and definition**

**3.**—(1) For the purposes of this Act, a **"platform service"** means any of the following, in so far as it is accessible by recipients in the United Kingdom—

- (a) a video-sharing platform service within the meaning of Schedule 15A of the Communications Act 2003;
- (b) a regulated television programme service within the meaning of section 362 of the Communications Act 2003;
- (c) an on-demand programme service within the meaning of section 368A of the Communications Act 2003;
- (d) a live-streaming service;
- (e) a podcast distribution service;
- (f) a social media service which permits the hosting or distribution of audio-visual content;
- (g) an immersive content platform; and
- (h) any other electronic service designated by the Secretary of State under section 5 of this Act.

(2) The Secretary of State shall by regulations update, amend, or extend the categories in subsection (1) as technological development may require, and any such regulations shall be subject to the affirmative resolution procedure.

---

**Subliminal content: definition and interpretation**

**4.**—(1) For the purposes of this Act, **"subliminal content"** means any audio-visual or other stimulus which—

- (a) is presented below the threshold of conscious perception of a reasonable recipient; and
- (b) is intended or reasonably likely to influence the thoughts, beliefs, attitudes, or behaviours of a recipient without their conscious awareness;

and includes, but is not limited to, images, sounds, words, or signals embedded within content in such a manner as to circumvent or bypass conscious cognitive processing.

(2) The prohibition in section 321(4) of the Communications Act 2003, which prohibits the inclusion of subliminal advertising in regulated broadcast services, shall be read together with the provisions of this Act and shall be construed as applying to all platform services within the meaning of section 3 of this Act.

---

**Power of Secretary of State to designate services**

**5.**—(1) The Secretary of State may, by order made by statutory instrument, designate any electronic service or category of service as a platform service for the purposes of this Act where the Secretary of State is satisfied that—

- (a) the service is accessible by recipients in the United Kingdom;
- (b) the service hosts, distributes, or may distribute hypnotic induction content or subliminal content; and
- (c) the application of this Act to such service is proportionate, having regard to the nature and reach of the service.

(2) Before making an order under subsection (1), the Secretary of State shall consult—

- (a) the Authority;
- (b) such bodies representative of operators as the Secretary of State considers appropriate;
- (c) such bodies representative of professional hypnotherapists as the Secretary of State considers appropriate; and
- (d) the Information Commissioner.

(3) An order under this section shall be subject to the affirmative resolution procedure.

---

## PART 2

### EXTENSION OF THE HYPNOTISM ACT 1952

**Application of the 1952 Act to digital media**

**6.**—(1) The Hypnotism Act 1952 is amended as follows.

(2) After section 1 insert—

> **"1A. Application to digital media and online services**
>
> *(1) The provisions of this Act shall apply, with the modifications set out in the Hypnotism (Digital Media and Online Services) Act 2024, to the demonstration or performance of hypnotism where such demonstration or performance is—*
>
> *(a) conducted by means of digital media as defined in section 2 of the 2024 Act;*
>
> *(b) transmitted, broadcast, hosted, or made available for access by recipients in the United Kingdom; or*
>
> *(c) presented as part of a live-streamed entertainment, theatrical, or public demonstration.*
>
> *(2) For the purposes of this section, a demonstration or performance of hypnotism which takes place physically before a live audience but which is simultaneously or subsequently transmitted by digital means shall be subject both to the provisions of this Act as originally enacted and to the provisions of the 2024 Act.*
>
> *(3) Nothing in this section shall be construed as limiting or diminishing the powers of local authorities under section 1 of this Act in relation to entertainment conducted at venues within their area, including where such entertainment is simultaneously live-streamed.*"

---

**Prohibition on hypnotic entertainment content without appropriate authorisation**

**7.**—(1) Subject to the exceptions in section 8, no operator of a platform service shall knowingly host, publish, transmit, distribute, or make available hypnotic induction content which constitutes or is part of a public entertainment demonstration of hypnotism unless—

- (a) such content has been authorised in accordance with this Act; and
- (b) such content complies with the content standards set out in Part 3 of this Act.

(2) For the purposes of subsection (1), a person **"knowingly"** hosts or distributes such content if—

- (a) the operator has actual knowledge of the nature of the content; or
- (b) the operator would have had such knowledge had it applied such monitoring, detection, or review procedures as are reasonable in all the circumstances and as may be required by the Authority under Part 3 of this Act.

(3) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence and is liable—

- (a) on summary conviction, to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum; or
- (b) on conviction on indictment, to a fine.

(4) Where an offence under this section is committed by a body corporate and it is proved to have been committed with the consent or connivance of, or to be attributable to any neglect on the part of—

- (a) a director, manager, secretary, or other similar officer of the body corporate; or
- (b) any person who was purporting to act in any such capacity,

that person as well as the body corporate shall be guilty of the offence and shall be liable to be proceeded against and punished accordingly.

---

**Exceptions and exemptions**

**8.**—(1) Section 7 does not apply to—

- (a) therapeutic content transmitted by a professional hypnotherapist directly to an individual recipient in the context of a private clinical or therapeutic relationship, where the individual recipient has given explicit and informed written consent prior to receiving such content;

- (b) educational content produced for the purposes of academic study, professional training in hypnotherapy, or scientific research, which is—
  - (i) not made available to the general public without age verification and professional qualification verification; and
  - (ii) accompanied by clear explanatory material identifying the nature and purpose of the content;

- (c) content produced for the purposes of a regulated medical or psychological intervention, conducted under the oversight of a registered medical practitioner or registered psychologist, and transmitted only to identified individual patients or participants in a regulated clinical trial;

- (d) content which has been specifically approved by the Authority for broadcast on a licensed television programme service under Part 3 of the Communications Act 2003, provided that such content complies with the conditions of the relevant licence and with any code issued by the Authority under section 319 of that Act, as supplemented by this Act.

(2) A person seeking to rely on an exception in subsection (1) shall bear the burden of demonstrating that the exception applies.

(3) The Secretary of State may by regulations made by statutory instrument—

- (a) add further exceptions to subsection (1); or
- (b) remove or modify exceptions in subsection (1),

provided that any such regulations are subject to the affirmative resolution procedure and that the Secretary of State has first consulted the Authority and such other persons as the Secretary of State considers appropriate.

---

## PART 3

### CONTENT STANDARDS, SAFEGUARDS AND REGULATORY POWERS

**Duty of the Authority to issue codes of practice**

**9.**—(1) The Authority shall, within eighteen months of the commencement of this Part, issue one or more codes of practice for the purposes of this Act (**"the Digital Hypnotic Content Codes"**), which shall set out—

- (a) the standards to be met by operators in relation to hypnotic induction content and subliminal content on platform services;
- (b) the technical and procedural protective safeguards to be implemented by operators;
- (c) requirements for age verification in relation to hypnotic induction content;
- (d) requirements for informed consent mechanisms in relation to such content;
- (e) standards for the labelling, classification, and disclosure of hypnotic induction content;
- (f) requirements relating to algorithmic amplification of hypnotic induction content, including restrictions on the unsolicited recommendation of such content to recipients who have not explicitly sought it;
- (g) requirements applicable to live-streamed hypnotic entertainment content; and
- (h) guidance on the interaction of this Act with the Authority's existing broadcasting codes issued under section 319 of the Communications Act 2003, and with the codes issued under the Online Safety Act 2023.

(2) Before issuing or revising a Digital Hypnotic Content Code, the Authority shall—

- (a) publish a draft of the proposed code or revision;
- (b) invite representations from operators, professional bodies, consumer groups, and any other interested persons within a period of not less than twelve weeks; and
- (c) have regard to those representations before finalising the code.

(3) A Digital Hypnotic Content Code shall not have effect until it has been approved by the Secretary of State, and the Secretary of State shall lay a copy of any such code before Parliament.

(4) Compliance with a Digital Hypnotic Content Code shall be taken into account by the Authority in determining whether an operator has complied with the duties imposed by this Part; but failure to comply with a code does not of itself give rise to civil or criminal liability.

---

**Protective safeguards: mandatory requirements for operators**

**10.**—(1) Every operator of a designated online service shall implement and maintain the following protective safeguards in respect of hypnotic induction content hosted on or distributed through that service—

- **(a) Content identification and labelling:** the operator shall ensure that hypnotic induction content is identified, labelled, and disclosed as such prior to a recipient accessing or viewing the content, in a manner that is—
  - (i) clear, prominent, and not misleading;
  - (ii) presented before the initiation of playback; and
  - (iii) accompanied by a description of the nature of the content sufficient to allow an informed decision by the recipient;

- **(b) Age verification:** the operator shall implement age verification measures to prevent access to hypnotic induction content by persons under the age of eighteen, in accordance with such technical standards as the Authority may specify;

- **(c) Informed consent mechanism:** the operator shall implement a consent mechanism whereby a recipient is required, prior to accessing hypnotic induction content, to—
  - (i) acknowledge that the content contains hypnotic induction material;
  - (ii) confirm that they are accessing the content voluntarily and with an awareness of its nature;
  - (iii) confirm that they are not accessing the content while operating a motor vehicle, heavy machinery, or in any other circumstance in which impairment of consciousness or attention could cause harm to themselves or others; and
  - (iv) confirm, where the Authority requires, that they do not suffer from any condition, including epilepsy, psychosis, or susceptibility to dissociative episodes, which may make the content harmful to them;

- **(d) Algorithmic restriction:** the operator shall ensure that hypnotic induction content is not—
  - (i) algorithmically recommended to any recipient who has not specifically and affirmatively sought such content; or
  - (ii) included in any autoplay, continuous play, or similar automated sequence without the explicit advance consent of the recipient;

- **(e) Opt-out and withdrawal mechanisms:** the operator shall provide recipients with a readily accessible means to—
  - (i) opt out of receiving recommendations for or notifications about hypnotic induction content; and
  - (ii) report hypnotic induction content which they consider to have been presented without appropriate disclosure;

- **(f) Content host verification:** where hypnotic induction content is hosted by a third party and distributed through the operator's platform, the operator shall take reasonable steps to verify that the content host is a professional hypnotherapist or otherwise a person authorised to produce such content under section 8 of this Act; and

- **(g) Record-keeping:** the operator shall maintain records sufficient to demonstrate compliance with this Act and shall make such records available to the Authority upon request.

(2) The Authority may by direction modify or supplement the requirements of subsection (1) in relation to a particular operator or category of operator, having regard to the size, nature, and risk profile of the operator's service.

(3) An operator who fails without reasonable excuse to comply with subsection (1) shall be liable to a financial penalty imposed by the Authority in accordance with section 15 of this Act.

---

**Special provisions for live-streamed hypnotic entertainment**

**11.**—(1) No person shall conduct a live-streamed demonstration of hypnotism for purposes of public entertainment unless—

- (a) not less than seven days before the live stream, that person has notified the Authority in writing of—
  - (i) the date, time, and anticipated duration of the live stream;
  - (ii) the name and qualifications of the person who will conduct the hypnosis;
  - (iii) the platform or platforms through which the live stream will be made available;
  - (iv) the nature and content of the proposed demonstration; and
  - (v) the protective safeguards which the platform operator has confirmed will be in place;

- (b) the operator of each platform through which the live stream is to be made available has confirmed in writing to that person that the safeguards required by section 10 of this Act will be implemented for the duration of the live stream; and

- (c) the live stream includes, at the commencement and at intervals of not less than fifteen minutes throughout, a prominent audio and visual notice to the effect that the content contains hypnotic induction material and that recipients who have concerns about their health or susceptibility should cease viewing.

(2) The requirement in subsection (1)(a) may be waived or reduced by the Authority in circumstances of reasonable urgency, subject to such conditions as the Authority considers appropriate.

(3) Where a live-streamed demonstration of hypnotism is also a licensable public entertainment within the meaning of the Licensing Act 2003 by reason of being conducted at a licensed venue, the requirements of that Act and of any relevant local authority licence condition shall continue to apply in addition to the requirements of this section.

(4) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence and is liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding level 5 on the standard scale.

---

**Prohibition on subliminal hypnotic content**

**12.**—(1) No person shall include, or cause to be included, in any digital media on any platform service any subliminal content which is intended or reasonably likely to induce hypnosis or a hypnotic state in a recipient, or to bypass the conscious awareness of a recipient for the purpose of influencing their conduct, beliefs, or attitudes.

(2) The prohibition in subsection (1) applies—

- (a) absolutely, with no exceptions, in relation to content transmitted to persons under the age of eighteen; and
- (b) subject to the exceptions in subsection (3), in relation to content transmitted to persons aged eighteen and over.

(3) Subsection (1) does not apply to subliminal content which—

- (a) forms part of a licensed clinical or therapeutic intervention and is transmitted only to consenting individual patients under the supervision of a registered medical practitioner or registered clinical psychologist; and
- (b) has been approved by the Authority prior to use.

(4) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence and is liable—

- (a) on summary conviction, to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum;
- (b) on conviction on indictment, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding two years, or a fine, or both.

---

**Prohibition on covert hypnotic techniques in advertising and commercial content**

**13.**—(1) No person shall employ covert hypnotic techniques in any advertisement or commercial communication transmitted by means of a platform service with the intention of, or with the likely effect of, influencing a recipient's purchasing decisions, political opinions, or other material life choices without that recipient's awareness.

(2) For the purposes of this section, an **"advertisement or commercial communication"** includes—

- (a) any advertisement within the meaning of the BCAP Code or the CAP Code as currently in force;
- (b) any sponsored or promoted content; and
- (c) any influencer marketing content.

(3) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence and is liable—

- (a) on summary conviction, to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum; or
- (b) on conviction on indictment, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding three years, or a fine, or both.

(4) The Authority may make provision in a Digital Hypnotic Content Code for the identification, review, and removal of content suspected of violating this section.

---

**Interaction with the Online Safety Act 2023**

**14.**—(1) Hypnotic induction content which causes or is likely to cause psychological harm to recipients shall be treated, for the purposes of the Online Safety Act 2023, as **"content that is harmful to adults"** within the meaning of that Act, and the duties imposed by Part 3 of the Online Safety Act 2023 in relation to such content shall apply accordingly.

(2) Hypnotic induction content accessible by persons under the age of eighteen shall be treated as **"primary priority content that is harmful to children"** for the purposes of section 62 of the Online Safety Act 2023 where—

- (a) such content has not been subject to the age verification measures required by section 10(1)(b) of this Act; or
- (b) the Authority has determined that such content poses a material risk of psychological harm to children.

(3) Where there is a conflict between the obligations imposed by the Online Safety Act 2023 and those imposed by this Act, the provisions imposing the higher standard of protection for recipients shall prevail, and the Authority shall have regard to this principle in the exercise of its functions.

(4) The Authority shall ensure co-ordination between its functions under this Act and its functions as the online safety regulator under the Online Safety Act 2023, including by issuing joint guidance where appropriate.

---

## PART 4

### ENFORCEMENT AND PENALTIES

**Enforcement powers of the Authority**

**15.**—(1) The Authority may take such enforcement action as is provided for in this Part where it has reasonable grounds to believe that an operator has contravened or is contravening the requirements of Part 3 of this Act.

(2) The Authority may issue a **Compliance Notice** to an operator, requiring that operator to—

- (a) take such specified steps as the Authority considers necessary to bring the operator into compliance; and
- (b) do so within such period as the Authority specifies, not being less than twenty-eight days from the date of the notice, except where the Authority determines that urgent action is required to prevent serious harm.

(3) The Authority may impose a **financial penalty** on an operator who—

- (a) fails to comply with a Compliance Notice within the specified period; or
- (b) has committed a serious, repeated, or deliberate contravention of Part 3 of this Act.

(4) The amount of a financial penalty under subsection (3) shall not exceed the greater of—

- (a) **£18,000,000**; or
- (b) **ten per cent of the operator's qualifying worldwide revenue**,

as the Authority may determine, having regard to the seriousness of the contravention, the size and resources of the operator, the extent of the harm caused or risked, and any steps taken by the operator to remedy the contravention.

(5) The Authority may apply to the High Court for an **Injunction** requiring an operator to—

- (a) cease hosting, transmitting, or making available specified content; or
- (b) implement specified technical measures without delay,

where the Authority considers that immediate action is necessary to prevent serious harm to recipients.

(6) In urgent cases where there is an imminent risk of significant harm, the Authority may by notice require an operator to—

- (a) immediately suspend access to specified hypnotic induction content pending a full review; and
- (b) notify affected recipients that access to specified content has been suspended and the reason for suspension.

(7) The Authority shall publish, in such manner as it considers appropriate, any Compliance Notice, financial penalty, or injunction issued or imposed under this section, subject to any redaction necessary to protect commercially sensitive information.

---

**Rights of appeal**

**16.**—(1) An operator upon whom the Authority has served a Compliance Notice or imposed a financial penalty under section 15 may appeal to the Competition Appeal Tribunal against—

- (a) the decision to impose the notice or penalty;
- (b) the amount of any financial penalty; or
- (c) the steps required by a Compliance Notice.

(2) An appeal under subsection (1) must be made within twenty-eight days of the date on which the Compliance Notice or notice of financial penalty was served, or such longer period as the Tribunal may in exceptional circumstances permit.

(3) On an appeal under this section, the Competition Appeal Tribunal may—

- (a) uphold the Authority's decision;
- (b) vary or modify the Compliance Notice or the amount of the financial penalty; or
- (c) set aside the Authority's decision in whole or in part.

(4) The bringing of an appeal under this section shall not, except by order of the Tribunal, stay the effect of the Compliance Notice or the obligation to pay the financial penalty.

---

**Prosecution and criminal enforcement**

**17.**—(1) Proceedings for offences under this Act may be instituted only by or with the consent of—

- (a) the Authority;
- (b) the Director of Public Prosecutions; or
- (c) in Scotland, the Lord Advocate.

(2) Where the Authority institutes proceedings for an offence under this Act, the Authority shall give notice to the Secretary of State not less than fourteen days before the proceedings are commenced.

(3) Proceedings for a summary offence under this Act shall not be commenced more than twelve months after the date on which the Authority first had sufficient evidence to justify a prosecution, provided that they may not in any event be commenced more than three years after the commission of the offence.

---

**Duties of internet service providers**

**18.**—(1) Where the Authority has determined that an operator established outside the United Kingdom is providing a platform service accessible in the United Kingdom in contravention of this Act, the Authority may direct any internet service provider operating in the United Kingdom to take such technical measures as the Authority considers proportionate and necessary to restrict access by recipients in the United Kingdom to specified content or to the operator's service.

(2) Before issuing a direction under subsection (1), the Authority shall—

- (a) give the operator such notice as is practicable in the circumstances;
- (b) invite representations from the operator within such period as the Authority specifies; and
- (c) consider whether measures short of restricting access could adequately address the contravention.

(3) An internet service provider which fails without reasonable excuse to comply with a direction under subsection (1) commits an offence and is liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum.

---

## PART 5

### ADVISORY AND CONSULTATIVE PROVISIONS

**Establishment of the Hypnotic Digital Content Advisory Panel**

**19.**—(1) The Authority shall establish and maintain a panel of persons (**"the Advisory Panel"**) to advise the Authority on the exercise of its functions under this Act.

(2) The Advisory Panel shall include—

- (a) not fewer than two persons who are professional hypnotherapists registered with a body listed in Schedule 2 to this Act;
- (b) not fewer than two persons with expertise in clinical psychology or psychiatry;
- (c) not fewer than one person with expertise in digital media technology;
- (d) not fewer than one person with expertise in consumer protection and online safety;
- (e) not fewer than one person experienced in representing the interests of recipients, including persons who may be vulnerable; and
- (f) not fewer than one person with expertise in broadcast regulation.

(3) The Secretary of State may by regulations made by statutory instrument amend the composition of the Advisory Panel.

(4) The Authority shall consult the Advisory Panel before issuing or revising a Digital Hypnotic Content Code under section 9.

(5) The Advisory Panel shall publish an annual report on the state of compliance with this Act and the adequacy of the regulatory framework, which shall be laid before Parliament by the Secretary of State.

---

**Research and review**

**20.**—(1) The Secretary of State shall commission an independent review of the operation and effectiveness of this Act within three years of the commencement of Part 3.

(2) The person or persons conducting the review shall consider—

- (a) whether the provisions of this Act have achieved the purpose of protecting recipients from harmful hypnotic content transmitted by digital means;
- (b) whether the regulatory framework remains proportionate in light of technological developments;
- (c) whether additional categories of content or service should be brought within the scope of this Act;
- (d) the effectiveness of the enforcement provisions; and
- (e) the impact of this Act on legitimate therapeutic, educational, and creative uses of hypnosis in digital media.

(3) The Secretary of State shall lay a report of the review before Parliament and shall publish a response to its recommendations within six months of receiving the report.

---

## PART 6

### GENERAL AND SUPPLEMENTARY

**Guidance**

**21.**—(1) The Authority may issue guidance to operators, professional hypnotherapists, and recipients about the operation of this Act, including guidance on—

- (a) what constitutes hypnotic induction content for the purposes of this Act;
- (b) the steps which operators may take to ensure compliance; and
- (c) the circumstances in which the exceptions in section 8 will be considered to apply.

(2) The Authority shall publish any guidance issued under this section and shall keep it under review.

(3) A court or tribunal considering any matter arising under this Act shall have regard to any relevant guidance issued by the Authority.

---

**International co-operation**

**22.**—(1) The Secretary of State may, with the approval of Parliament, enter into arrangements with governments of other countries or territories for—

- (a) the recognition of equivalent regulatory frameworks in relation to hypnotic content in digital media;
- (b) the sharing of enforcement information between regulatory authorities;
- (c) the co-ordination of action against operators established outside the United Kingdom who transmit hypnotic content to recipients in the United Kingdom in contravention of this Act; and
- (d) mutual assistance in the investigation of suspected offences.

(2) In exercising its functions under this Act, the Authority shall have regard to the regulatory approaches adopted in other jurisdictions and to any international standards or guidance relevant to the regulation of hypnotic content in digital media.

---

**Saving provisions and relationship with other legislation**

**23.**—(1) Nothing in this Act shall be construed as limiting or restricting the powers of a local authority under section 1 of the Hypnotism Act 1952 in relation to public entertainments within its area.

(2) The provisions of this Act shall be read together with, and shall not derogate from, the provisions of—

- (a) the Communications Act 2003;
- (b) the Online Safety Act 2023;
- (c) the Licensing Act 2003;
- (d) the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974;
- (e) the Consumer Protection from Unfair Trading Regulations 2008 (SI 2008/1277); and
- (f) the UK General Data Protection Regulation and the Data Protection Act 2018, in particular as regards the processing of health data obtained through consent mechanisms under section 10(1)(c) of this Act.

(3) Where conduct is regulated both by this Act and by the Online Safety Act 2023, the Authority shall adopt a co-ordinated approach to enforcement so as to avoid duplication of proceedings.

---

**Regulations and orders**

**24.**—(1) Any power to make regulations or orders under this Act is exercisable by statutory instrument.

(2) A statutory instrument containing regulations subject to the affirmative resolution procedure may not be made unless a draft of the instrument has been laid before and approved by a resolution of each House of Parliament.

(3) A statutory instrument containing regulations subject to the negative resolution procedure is subject to annulment in pursuance of a resolution of either House of Parliament.

---

**Financial provisions**

**25.**—(1) There shall be paid out of money provided by Parliament—

- (a) any expenditure incurred by the Secretary of State under this Act;
