# Converting Struct to Vec or HashMap in Rust

## Using a HashMap

### Manual Implementation
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

struct Person {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
    city: String,
}

impl Person {
    fn to_hashmap(&self) -> HashMap<String, String> {
        let mut map = HashMap::new();
        map.insert("name".to_string(), self.name.clone());
        map.insert("age".to_string(), self.age.to_string());
        map.insert("city".to_string(), self.city.clone());
        map
    }
}

fn main() {
    let person = Person {
        name: "Alice".to_string(),
        age: 30,
        city: "NYC".to_string(),
    };

    let map = person.to_hashmap();
    println!("{:?}", map);
}
```

### Using `From` / `Into` Traits
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

struct Person {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
}

impl From<Person> for HashMap<String, String> {
    fn from(p: Person) -> Self {
        let mut map = HashMap::new();
        map.insert("name".to_string(), p.name);
        map.insert("age".to_string(), p.age.to_string());
        map
    }
}

fn main() {
    let person = Person { name: "Alice".to_string(), age: 30 };

    let map: HashMap<String, String> = person.into(); // uses Into automatically
    println!("{:?}", map);
}
```

---

## Using a Vec

### Vec of Tuples
```rust
struct Person {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
    city: String,
}

impl Person {
    fn to_vec(&self) -> Vec<(String, String)> {
        vec![
            ("name".to_string(), self.name.clone()),
            ("age".to_string(), self.age.to_string()),
            ("city".to_string(), self.city.clone()),
        ]
    }
}
```

### Vec of Values Only
```rust
impl Person {
    fn values_vec(&self) -> Vec<String> {
        vec![
            self.name.clone(),
            self.age.to_string(),
            self.city.clone(),
        ]
    }
}
```

---

## Using Serde (Most Practical for Complex Structs)

Add to `Cargo.toml`:
```toml
[dependencies]
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
serde_json = "1"
```

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize};
use serde_json;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Person {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
    city: String,
}

fn main() {
    let person = Person {
        name: "Alice".to_string(),
        age: 30,
        city: "NYC".to_string(),
    };

    // Struct -> HashMap
    let map: HashMap<String, serde_json::Value> = 
        serde_json::from_value(serde_json::to_value(&person).unwrap()).unwrap();

    println!("{:?}", map);
    // {"name": "Alice", "age": 30, "city": "NYC"}
}
```

---

## Summary

| Method | Best For |
|--------|----------|
| Manual `to_hashmap()` | Simple, no dependencies |
| `From`/`Into` traits | Idiomatic Rust conversions |
| `to_vec()` | Ordered key-value pairs |
| **Serde** | Complex/nested structs, production use |

The **Serde approach** is generally recommended for real-world use cases as it handles nested structs, enums, and various types automatically.

